The Security Council,
Recalling its resolutions 1970 (2011), 1973 (2011), 2009 (2011), 2146 (2014), 2292 (2016), 2357 (2017), 2420 (2018), 2473 (2019), 2526 (2020), 2578 (2021), 2635 (2022), 2684 (2023), 2701 (2023), 2733 (2024), 2755 (2024) and 2769 (2025), and the statements of its President on Libya,
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and national unity of Libya,
Reaffirming its strong commitment to an inclusive, Libyan-led and Libyan-owned political process facilitated by the United Nations that builds on progress achieved in negotiations thus far and enables the holding of free, fair, transparent and inclusive presidential and parliamentary elections across Libya as soon as possible and the formation of a unified Libyan government,
Welcoming the continued efforts of the United Nations Support Mission in Libya and its Special Representative of the Secretary-General, and calling on all Libyan actors and institutions to engage constructively, in good faith and without preconditions with United Nations facilitation,
Expressing grave concern about the clashes that erupted in Tripoli on 12 May 2025, the continued fragility of the security situation and the growing influence of armed groups, and underlining the urgent need for de-escalation, restraint, protection of civilians and unhindered humanitarian access,
Recalling the 23 October 2020 ceasefire agreement and urging all parties to implement it in full, including through the withdrawal of all foreign forces, foreign fighters and mercenaries from Libya without further delay,
Reaffirming the arms embargo established by paragraphs 9 and 10 of resolution 1970 (2011), as modified by subsequent resolutions, and expressing grave concern at continued violations of the arms embargo and at the destabilizing accumulation and transfer of arms and related materiel in Libya,
Noting the Panel of Experts’ finding that the arms embargo remains ineffective where Member States control logistics flows and supply chains to armed actors in Libya, and demanding full compliance by all Member States and all Libyan parties,
Recalling resolutions 2292 (2016), 2357 (2017), 2420 (2018), 2473 (2019), 2526 (2020), 2578 (2021), 2635 (2022), 2684 (2023) and 2733 (2024), which authorized, for the period specified therein, inspection on the high seas off the coast of Libya of vessels bound to or from Libya believed to be carrying arms or related materiel in violation of the relevant Security Council resolutions, and the seizure and disposal of such items,
Recalling that international law, as reflected in the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea of 10 December 1982, sets out the legal framework applicable to activities in the oceans and seas,
Stressing the need to prevent and counter migrant smuggling and human trafficking, and emphasizing the obligation of all States to protect the human rights of migrants and refugees, in accordance with applicable international law,
Expressing concern that the illicit export of petroleum, including crude oil and refined petroleum products, from Libya undermines the Government of Libya and the National Oil Corporation and poses a threat to the peace, security and stability of Libya,
Reaffirming that all parties must comply with their obligations under international humanitarian law and international human rights law, as applicable, and emphasizing the importance of accountability for violations or abuses,
Determining that the situation in Libya continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,
Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,
1. Reaffirms the arms embargo established by paragraphs 9 and 10 of resolution 1970 (2011), as modified by subsequent resolutions, and demands full compliance by all Member States and all Libyan parties;
2. Deplores the recent clashes in Tripoli and all acts of violence in Libya, calls on all parties to exercise maximum restraint, refrain from any action that could escalate tensions or undermine the political process, and ensure the protection of civilians and unhindered humanitarian access;
3. Calls on all Libyan actors and institutions to refrain from unilateral actions, to recommit to de-escalation and good-faith dialogue under United Nations auspices, and to take urgent steps towards the holding of free, fair, transparent and inclusive presidential and parliamentary elections and the formation of a unified Libyan government;
4. Reaffirms its full support for the United Nations Support Mission in Libya and its Special Representative of the Secretary-General, and calls on all parties to cooperate fully with the Mission, including by ensuring the safety and security of its personnel and unhindered access throughout Libya;
5. Calls on all parties to implement the 23 October 2020 ceasefire agreement in full, including through the withdrawal of all foreign forces, foreign fighters and mercenaries from Libya without further delay, and requests the Secretary-General to include in his regular reporting to the Council any significant developments in this regard;
6. Reiterates that all Member States shall take the necessary measures to prevent the direct or indirect supply, sale or transfer to Libya of arms and related materiel, including by preventing transfers to armed groups, and calls on Member States to report violations of the arms embargo to the Security Council Committee established pursuant to resolution 1970 (2011);
7. Authorizes Member States, acting nationally or through regional organizations or arrangements, and in cooperation with the Government of Libya, to inspect on the high seas off the coast of Libya vessels bound to or from Libya, which they have reasonable grounds to believe are carrying arms or related materiel to or from Libya in violation of the arms embargo, for a period of 12 months from the date of adoption of this resolution, provided that Member States make good faith efforts to first obtain the consent of the vessel’s flag State prior to any inspections, and authorizes such Member States to seize and dispose of such items, including by destruction, and directs the Committee to be informed immediately of any such inspections and seizures;
8. Calls on all flag States to cooperate with such inspections and seizures, and calls on Member States and regional organizations to coordinate closely, including through the exchange of information, in order to enhance implementation of the arms embargo and maritime security off the coast of Libya;
9. Requests the Panel of Experts, in accordance with its mandate, to include in its reports information on the implementation of paragraph 7 of this resolution, including cases of non-cooperation and violations, and requests the Secretary-General to include in his regular reporting to the Council any significant developments relating to the implementation of this resolution;
10. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

