1. Reaffirms the arms embargo established by paragraphs 9 and 10 of resolution 1970 (2011), as modified by subsequent resolutions, and demands full compliance by all Member States and all Libyan parties;
2. Condemns the recent clashes in Tripoli and all acts of violence in Libya, including the assassination of Abdel Ghani al-Kikli, deplores the continued fragility of the security situation, calls on all parties to exercise maximum restraint, refrain from any action that could escalate tensions or undermine the political process, and ensure the protection of civilians and safe, rapid and unhindered humanitarian access to all persons in need, and stresses the need for prompt, independent, impartial and transparent investigations into all violations and abuses, including the clashes of 12 May 2025, and accountability for those responsible;
3. Calls on all Libyan actors and institutions to refrain from unilateral actions, to recommit to de-escalation and good-faith dialogue under United Nations auspices, and to take urgent steps towards the holding of free, fair, transparent and inclusive presidential and parliamentary elections and the formation of a unified Libyan government, and stresses the importance of the full, equal, meaningful and safe participation of women and youth in the political process and elections;
4. Welcomes the adoption by the House of Representatives of the national reconciliation and transitional justice law and calls for its inclusive and transparent implementation in accordance with international human rights standards;
5. Reaffirms its full support for the United Nations Support Mission in Libya and its Special Representative of the Secretary-General, and calls on all parties to cooperate fully with the Mission, including by ensuring the safety and security of its personnel and unhindered access throughout Libya;
6. Requests the United Nations Support Mission in Libya, in coordination with the African Union and the League of Arab States, to support Libyan-led national reconciliation and transitional justice efforts, including implementation of the national reconciliation and transitional justice law;
7. Calls on all parties to implement the 23 October 2020 ceasefire agreement in full, including through the withdrawal of all foreign forces, foreign fighters and mercenaries from Libya without further delay, in a manner that respects Libya’s sovereignty and is coordinated with the Government of Libya and neighboring States, and requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within 90 days on progress, including information on steps taken to implement the ceasefire and the withdrawal of foreign forces, foreign fighters and mercenaries, in coordination with the Government of Libya and neighboring States;
8. Welcomes the continued work of the 5+5 Joint Military Commission and the two Chiefs of Staff, urges continued efforts towards the reunification of Libyan military and security institutions, and calls on all parties to engage constructively and on Member States to support those Libyan-led efforts, in line with resolution 2769 (2025);
9. Reiterates that all Member States shall take the necessary measures to prevent the direct or indirect supply, sale or transfer to Libya of arms and related materiel, including by preventing transfers to armed groups, including through land, sea and air routes, and calls on Member States to cooperate fully with the Panel of Experts and relevant regional maritime operations in this regard, and to report violations of the arms embargo to the Security Council Committee established pursuant to resolution 1970 (2011); recalls that individuals and entities determined by the Committee to have violated the arms embargo, or assisted others in doing so, are subject to designation; and affirms that nothing in this paragraph shall affect the provision of assistance to the Government of Libya as notified or approved in accordance with relevant Security Council resolutions;
10. Decides that the measures reaffirmed in paragraph 9 shall be without prejudice to the provision of humanitarian assistance and other activities consistent with resolution 2664 (2022), and calls on all Member States to ensure the prompt and full implementation of the humanitarian exemption;
11. Calls on all Member States to implement fully resolution 2769 (2025), including the measures concerning illicit exports and imports of petroleum and refined petroleum products, and reiterates that individuals or entities engaged in or providing support for acts that threaten the peace, stability or security of Libya, including through the illicit exploitation of crude oil or any other natural resources, may be designated for targeted sanctions;
12. Expresses concern about the high terrorism risk in Libya and the threat posed by terrorist groups, notes the need to strengthen Libyan counterterrorism, border and maritime security capabilities, and calls on Member States, at the request of the Libyan authorities, to consider providing support to strengthen those capabilities, including through international support as requested by the Presidential Council, in accordance with international law and resolution 2769 (2025);
13. Authorizes Member States, acting nationally or through regional organizations or arrangements, as appropriate, and after prior notification to and in close cooperation and coordination with the Government of Libya, in coordination with the Committee established pursuant to resolution 1970 (2011), and, as appropriate, regional organizations and the United Nations Support Mission in Libya, and in accordance with international law, including the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea and applicable human rights and refugee law, and in full respect of Libya’s sovereignty and maritime rights, to inspect on the high seas off the coast of Libya vessels bound to or from Libya which they have reasonable grounds to believe are carrying arms or related materiel in violation of the arms embargo, provided that inspections of vessels bound from Libya shall be limited to situations where there are reasonable grounds to believe that such vessels are carrying arms or related materiel in violation of the arms embargo and shall not interfere with lawful petroleum exports, for a period of six months from the date of adoption of this resolution, provided that Member States make good faith efforts to first obtain the consent of the vessel’s flag State prior to any inspection, consistent with resolutions 2292 (2016) and subsequent relevant resolutions, and that any inspection, seizure or disposal is conducted in full conformity with international law, including the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea, with due regard for the rights and obligations of flag States, the safety and treatment of all persons on board, and without unduly interfering with lawful commercial shipping or humanitarian assistance, and provided further that nothing in this paragraph authorizes the use of force; authorizes such Member States to seize such items, and decides that disposal of such items shall be coordinated with the Committee established pursuant to resolution 1970 (2011) and conducted in accordance with international law and with the consent of the flag State, and directs the Committee to be informed immediately of any such inspections and seizures and kept fully informed of disposal arrangements; further decides that this authorization shall be subject to review after six months and that inspections and seizures under this paragraph shall be limited to the implementation of the arms embargo and shall not be used for migration control or other purposes;
14. Calls on all flag States to cooperate with such inspections and seizures, and calls on Member States and regional organizations and arrangements, as appropriate, to coordinate closely, including through the exchange of information, in order to enhance implementation of the arms embargo and maritime security off the coast of Libya, and stresses that implementation of paragraph 13 shall not unduly interfere with lawful commercial shipping or humanitarian assistance;
15. Stresses that the authorization in paragraph 13 shall be implemented without prejudice to the sovereignty, independence and territorial integrity of Libya and shall not affect the normal export of petroleum by Libya;
16. Requests the Panel of Experts, in accordance with its mandate, to include in its reports information on the implementation of paragraph 13 of this resolution, including cases of non-cooperation and violations, and requests the Secretary-General to include in his regular reporting to the Council any significant developments relating to the implementation of this resolution, including a report on paragraph 13 within six months;
17. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.
